WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.640
Now that we've seen a real-life example, let's break down exactly how you assess someone to see if they need your help.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.880
Think back to the video we just watched. What did you see the rescuer do first?

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.920
She was starting the four key steps of assessment and activation that you see here on the screen.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.440
Step one is checking for responsiveness.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.680
To do this correctly, you want to tap the person firmly on both shoulders and ask loudly,

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.760
Are you okay?

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.879
When we say someone is responsive,

00:00:31.879 --> 00:00:39.360
we mean they move, speak, blink, or give you some kind of reaction. If they show any of these signs of life,

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:44.200
they are alive and do not need CPR, but they might still need medical attention

00:00:44.639 --> 00:00:50.040
through a call to 911. If you tap and shout and get no response at all,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.799
that is your signal that something is very wrong. As your instructor models this for you now,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.480
notice the firmness of the tap and the clarity of the shout.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.599
Once you have determined that the person is not responding, it is time for step two. Shout for help,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.400
call 911, and get an AED. If there are people around you,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.959
it is very important to avoid generic statements like, someone call 911. In a high-stress moment,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.400
people often need a direct command to take action.

00:01:21.639 --> 00:01:25.680
You should point to a specific person and tell them to call 911,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.519
then point to another specific person and tell them to find an AED.

00:01:30.839 --> 00:01:35.480
Now, what if you ask a stranger to get an AED and they do not know what it is?

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:42.040
Just tell them to ask around or look for one, and if they find it, to bring it back to you.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:50.160
They might not find one, but delegating that task allows you to stay focused on the person in need. If you are completely by yourself,

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.760
call 911 on your own phone immediately and put it on speakerphone.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.800
This is essential because it allows you to begin CPR while following the dispatcher's instructions.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.800
You might wonder if you should leave the person to go find an AED.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:11.399
You should only leave to retrieve an AED if you know exactly where it is and can get back very quickly.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.759
Never go on a search mission and leave the person unattended. Your main priority is to stay right there and provide care.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.360
Now we move to step three, which is checking for breathing by using a simple method.

00:02:26.759 --> 00:02:29.360
Look, listen, and feel.

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:34.199
Start by looking closely at the person's chest to see if it is rising and falling.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:41.199
At the same time, place your ear and cheek near their mouth and nose so you can listen and feel for any air moving.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.720
Now, this part is critical. If the person is only gasping, that is not normal breathing.

00:02:48.279 --> 00:02:54.080
We call this agonal breathing, and it often sounds like weak, irregular snorting or gasping.

00:02:54.559 --> 00:03:00.559
If you see or hear this, do not mistake it for normal breathing. You must begin CPR immediately.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.759
If they are breathing normally, they do not need CPR.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.759
In that case, you should wait for help to arrive, and you can gently roll them onto their left side into the recovery position.

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:15.360
As we practice this on our mannequins right now, I want you to really get down there.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:23.000
Lean in close, put your ear to the mannequin's mouth, and keep your eyes on the chest so you can feel for that rise and fall.

00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:35.000
You might have heard about checking for a pulse, but for lay rescuers, we do not advise this as it can be difficult to do correctly in a high-stress moment and uses up valuable time.

00:03:36.199 --> 00:03:42.600
Now we have reached step four, the most critical part of our rescue, beginning chest compressions.

00:03:43.039 --> 00:03:48.240
By now, you have determined the person is both unresponsive and not breathing normally.

00:03:48.639 --> 00:03:51.240
That is your absolute green light to act.

00:03:51.639 --> 00:03:58.639
If they aren't responding and they aren't breathing, their heart is not pumping, and they need you to do that job for them manually.

00:03:59.039 --> 00:04:03.240
Think of chest compressions as manually pumping the heart from the outside.

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:10.240
It is the act of rhythmically pressing down on the center of the chest to circulate blood to the brain and lungs.

